title: "The Auto-Debit at Three A.M."
date: "2026-08-13"
excerpt: "On a warm Wednesday morning in the second week of August, at three-oh-two in the morning, in a small one-bedroom on the fifteenth floor of a converted warehouse on Blake Street in downtown Denver, a thirty-two-year-old cloud-infrastructure engineer named Jared David Petrilli reads a plain email from Ally Bank confirming the next scheduled monthly transfer — $175, on the fifteenth of every month, to *WILLMAR COMMUNITY COLLEGE FOUNDATION, WILLMAR MN, memo — thank you* — the same auto-debit he set up on a Saturday morning in the April of 2016 and has not, in the one hundred and twenty-four months since, missed a single time. Somewhere on the second floor of a low red-brick building on Litchfield Avenue in Willmar, on the same Wednesday morning at nine-forty-one, the record for J. D. Petrilli in the CRM of the Willmar Community College Foundation reads *monthly, $175, lifetime giving $21,700, no degree, no engagement, no visits, do not contact.* A short letter, with a great deal of tenderness, on the plain typewritten note the college's registrar mailed a widowed nursing assistant on a cold Tuesday afternoon two weeks before Christmas of 2005 — and on the twenty-year promise a fifth-grader made in the front seat of a maroon Ford Aerostar that the industry has been quietly, patiently, without any malice at all, failing to see for eleven Aprils in a row."
