# The Auto-Debit at Three A.M.

The email lands in Jared Petrilli's inbox at three-oh-two on the second Wednesday of August.

*Scheduled transfer confirmed — $175.00, RECURRING, to WILLMAR COMMUNITY COLLEGE FOUNDATION, next posting date August 15.*

He does not open it. He does not need to. He has been getting the same email on the eleventh of every month for one hundred and twenty-four months, and the only reason he has not turned the notification off is that once, on a delayed flight into Minneapolis on the twenty-third of December of 2019, the little Ally line dropped down onto the lock screen of his phone in the aisle seat of an Airbus at seven-forty-one p.m. Central time, and he sat with his forehead against the small oval window for eleven quiet minutes, and decided, in that eleventh minute, that if he ever stopped needing to be reminded of it, he was going to be somebody he did not want to be.

He is thirty-two.

He is awake at three in the morning because a small alerting job on a Kubernetes cluster in Oregon has paged him for the third time this month, and because the pinned tab in the small square kitchen is a spreadsheet from his RSU broker that vested, on the last Thursday of July, at three million four hundred thousand dollars after tax.

He has not, in the fifteen days since, told anyone.

## What Loretta signed in the fall of 2004

Loretta Rose Petrilli, née Kolski, thirty-four years old, three months a widow, drove the eleven miles from the small rented duplex on Northwest Second Street in Willmar, Minnesota to the low red-brick building on Litchfield Avenue on a warm Wednesday afternoon in the second week of September of 2004, in a fifteen-year-old maroon Ford Aerostar with a broken air conditioner and a cracked windshield her husband David had been meaning to replace since the second Saturday of April.

She parked in the third row of the west lot.

She walked, in a plain cream cardigan she bought on the clearance rack at Herberger's in the March of 2001, to the small oak counter in the front office of the registrar, and signed a two-page enrollment form for the Practical Nursing certificate program at Willmar Community College.

The certificate cost, on the day she signed the form, two thousand three hundred and forty dollars.

She paid it, on the day she signed, in eleven money orders from the KeyBank branch on First Street South, in installments of two hundred and twelve dollars each.

Her husband David Anthony Petrilli, thirty-six, roofer, had gone through the second-floor rafters of a hog barn on a small feedlot three miles south of Prinsburg on a warm Tuesday morning in the last week of June of that year. The foreman, a fifty-one-year-old man named Delbert Anselm who had made two of these calls before, called Loretta at the Cenex on Highway 71 at nine-fifty-one and said what he had, on a slip of paper in the pocket of his work shirt, decided he was going to say.

Their son Jared was eleven.

He would turn twelve, in the small yellow kitchen of the duplex on Northwest Second Street, with a Betty Crocker yellow cake his neighbor Marlys Braaten baked and carried across the driveway in a Corningware dish, on a Sunday afternoon in the second week of October.

Loretta finished the Practical Nursing program on a snowy Friday in the second week of December of 2005.

She was, on the day she finished, three hundred dollars short.

## The note the registrar mailed on a Tuesday in the November of 2005

Marian Kolseth Braun, at that time forty-eight, at that time in the sixth of her twenty-nine years at the Willmar Community College registrar's office, opened the buff manila folder marked *Petrilli, L. R.* on a cold Tuesday afternoon in the second week of November of 2005.

The balance line read *$300.00 — final tuition installment due 12/1/2005.*

Marian read the line twice.

She got up from her desk. She walked to the second-to-last drawer of the second gray filing cabinet in the back of the registrar's office. She pulled the intake form Loretta had filled out on the Wednesday in September of 2004, on which the box marked *dependents* read *Jared, 11, 5th grade Roosevelt Elementary,* and on which the box marked *household income* read *$18,400 (Cenex cashier, part-time),* and on which the box marked *emergency contact* read *neighbor — Marlys Braaten, 320-235-4419.*

She sat back down at her desk.

She rolled a plain sheet of Willmar Community College letterhead into the IBM Selectric she kept, in the winter of 2005, on the small side table by the north window.

She typed one paragraph:

*Dear Mrs. Petrilli — I have credited the balance of your final installment to Chapter 205 hardship funds effective today. Your final transcript will be released on 12/16/2005 with a balance of $0.00. We would rather you have your Christmas. Warmly, Marian K. Braun, Registrar.*

She mailed it, at the blue drop box at the corner of Fourth and Litchfield on her way home, on a Tuesday afternoon at four-fifty-two.

Loretta read it on Wednesday morning at the small kitchen table on Northwest Second Street.

She read it, then she read it again, then she folded it in half and put it in the plain white envelope in the top drawer of the sideboard where she kept David's obituary from the *West Central Tribune* and the certificate of live birth for Jared David Petrilli from the June of 1993.

She did not tell Jared for eight days.

She told him on a Thursday evening in the second week of December, in the front seat of the Aerostar, in the parking lot of the Cash Wise on Highway 71, over an eight-piece box of chicken tenders and a large sweet tea to split, in the small quiet way a mother tells her son the specific size of the small warm miracle a stranger has just done for them.

Jared was twelve.

He asked, on the drive home along Highway 71 in the second-hand snow, whether the letter was still going to be in the drawer.

## What Jared did on a Saturday in the April of 2016

Jared graduated from Willmar Senior High in the June of 2011, from the University of Minnesota Duluth in the May of 2015 with a plain B.S. in computer science, and moved, on a Saturday morning in the second week of August of 2015, into a small first-floor studio in a two-story wood-frame house on Fourteenth Avenue in Northeast Minneapolis.

His first job, at a small B2B security software company on the third floor of a converted warehouse in the North Loop, paid, in the first year, sixty-seven thousand dollars.

On a Saturday morning in the second week of April of 2016 — eight months after the first paycheck, three months after he had finished paying off the last of his mother's dental bill from a bad molar she had put off since the winter of 2014 — he opened the small consumer bank app on his phone, tapped *Recurring Transfer,* typed *Willmar Community College Foundation* into the payee field, entered the routing and account numbers he had copied off the small *give-to-Willmar* page of the college's website onto a yellow Post-it stuck to the drywall above the little Ikea desk, and set the amount at $175.

He picked $175 because it was, on his rent-plus-groceries budget in the North Loop in the April of 2016, the largest number he could commit to without ever having to stop.

He set the recurrence to *monthly, indefinitely.*

He set the memo line to *thank you.*

He did not tell his mother.

He does not, on this Wednesday morning in the August of 2026, at three-oh-two, still, plan to tell his mother.

## What the CRM sees on the second floor of Litchfield Avenue

At nine-forty-one on the same Wednesday morning, in the small three-person foundation office on the second floor of the same low red-brick building on Litchfield Avenue in Willmar — Marian Kolseth Braun's old registrar's office is now the copy room down the hall; her Selectric was sold at the March of 2018 surplus sale to a small antique typewriter shop in St. Cloud — the incoming development director, a forty-one-year-old former sixth-grade social studies teacher named Bethany Halverson Nguyen, hired in the March of 2023 out of ISD 347 in Willmar, opens the third-quarter *lapsed / non-alumni monthly donors* tab of the foundation's Blackbaud dashboard.

Jared's line is on the fourteenth row.

*PETRILLI, JARED D. — 411 Blake St, Denver CO 80202. Monthly ACH since 4/2016. Lifetime giving $21,700. No degree conferred. Wealth screen: NOT RUN (non-alumnus, deprioritized). No engagement. Do not contact.*

Bethany reads the line the way anyone reads the fourteenth of forty-two lines on a Wednesday morning.

She scrolls.

## The wrong question the industry has been asking for twenty years

For twenty years, the category of software the Willmar Community College Foundation has been sold — at increasingly expensive annual contracts, on second-quarter renewal calls in overheated conference rooms — has been asked, and has answered, one question:

*Of our alumni, whose wealth-screen band changed this quarter?*

Jared is not an alumnus.

Jared has never set foot in a classroom on Litchfield Avenue. Jared has been on the *do-not-contact* tab, in one form or another, since a Saturday morning in the April of 2016.

The tool has been asked the wrong question.

The question the tool would have needed to answer, on a Saturday morning in the April of 2016 or on a Wednesday morning in the August of 2026 — either would have done — is smaller, and harder, and almost never appears on a keynote slide at a sector conference in a hotel across from an airport:

*Of the plain typewritten notes our registrar mailed on Tuesday afternoons in the November of 2005, is there a fifth-grader on Northwest Second Street who has been, on the fifteenth of every month since a Saturday morning in the April of 2016, quietly paying one back — and who is, in the last week of July of 2026, about to have a great deal more to pay it back with?*

That is not a wealth screen.

That is not a portfolio dashboard.

That is not a predictive-affinity model.

That is a small careful set of notes, assembled by somebody who has spent a Tuesday morning listening — to the plain *West Central Tribune* obituary for David Anthony Petrilli of Willmar from the last week of June of 2004, to the small paragraph on page nine of the December 16, 2005, *West Central Tribune* naming Loretta R. Petrilli among that fall's twenty-two graduates of the Willmar Community College Practical Nursing program, to the plain retirement notice for M. K. Braun of Willmar in the second week of June of 2022, to the small Series B TechCrunch line in the March of 2024 for the North Loop security company whose staff-engineer page still lists Jared David Petrilli under *Infrastructure,* and to a small acquisition rumor in the second week of July of 2026 on The Information that the same little B2B security company has just been quietly picked up by a large one.

## What Rōmy would put on Bethany's desk at nine-forty-two

At nine-forty-two on the same Wednesday morning, Rōmy puts on Bethany Nguyen's desk a single sourced page.

*J. D. Petrilli, our $175/month monthly since 4/2016 (non-alumnus, currently flagged do-not-contact), is the son of Loretta Rose Petrilli, one of our fall 2005 Practical Nursing graduates. In the November of 2005, our then-registrar Marian K. Braun (retired 6/2022, now living in Northfield, still on our emeritus courtesy list) waived Mrs. Petrilli's final $300 tuition installment under the Chapter 205 hardship line and mailed her a plain typewritten note that read, in full, "we would rather you have your Christmas." Mr. Petrilli, then eleven, is now thirty-two, a staff engineer at a Minneapolis-based B2B security company whose recent acquisition rumor is on The Information as of 7/12/2026. His first recurring gift to the foundation posted 4/15/2016, three months after his first job's first raise. Suggested: one plain cream card, this week, in your own hand — not from Development, from you — to Jared at Blake Street, mentioning his mother by name once, mentioning Marian by name once, mentioning the November of 2005 once. No ask. No case for support. Ask, at the very end, whether he would drive up to Willmar some Wednesday morning in October and talk, for twenty minutes, to our incoming class of thirty-two Practical Nursing students — about what a small note from a registrar can look like from the other side of a kitchen table on a Thursday evening in the December of 2005. Would you like a draft, in your own voice?*

Bethany reads the page twice.

She closes the Blackbaud tab.

She writes the card by hand on a Monday afternoon and mails it, from the post office on West Fourth Street, on a Tuesday.

## We are not the note in the drawer

Rōmy is not the plain typewritten paragraph a forty-eight-year-old registrar rolled into an IBM Selectric on a cold Tuesday afternoon in the second week of November of 2005.

Rōmy is not the eight-piece box of chicken tenders on the front seat of a maroon Ford Aerostar in the parking lot of the Cash Wise on Highway 71 on a Thursday evening in the December of 2005, or the yellow cake Marlys Braaten carried across the driveway in a Corningware dish, or the yellow Post-it stuck to the drywall above a small Ikea desk on Fourteenth Avenue in Northeast Minneapolis in the April of 2016, or the eleven quiet minutes a thirty-two-year-old cloud engineer spent with his forehead against the small oval window of an Airbus on a delayed flight into Minneapolis on the twenty-third of December of 2019.

Rōmy is the small careful page a good listener would have put on Bethany Halverson Nguyen's desk at nine-forty-two on a warm Wednesday morning in the August of 2026, if a good listener had been sitting on the small oak counter of the registrar's office on Litchfield Avenue on a Tuesday afternoon in the November of 2005, and in the front seat of the maroon Aerostar in the Cash Wise parking lot on a Thursday evening the following month, and beside the yellow Post-it on Fourteenth Avenue on the second Saturday of April in eleven Aprils in a row.

The whole product is a smaller, warmer job than the industry has been trying to sell you for twenty years.

It is not to prospect Jared.

It is to *notice* him.

## A small assignment for the officer reading this on a Sunday

Before the last of the sweet corn is off the trucks at the farmers' market this summer, do one small unglamorous thing this week.

Pull, out of your CRM, your ten longest-tenured *non-alumnus, monthly-recurring, do-not-contact* donors. Not the tier-ones. Not the wealth-screened ones. The kind whose lifetime line reads *no degree conferred, no engagement, no visits* — and whose first gift posted on the second Saturday of some April five, eight, eleven years ago, for whatever number, on their rent-plus-groceries budget in the North Loop or the Mission or Old Town or East Rock or Wicker Park, they could commit to without ever having to stop.

Look up, in the local paper of the town where each of them grew up, the fall of the year their gift began, and the six months before.

You will find, on more Tuesday afternoons than the sector has been telling you, a thirty-two-year-old somewhere quietly returning a small typewritten kindness a woman named Marian mailed on her way home from work in the November of 2005 — a note whose whole content was *we would rather you have your Christmas,* and whose whole cost, on the ledger of a small community college in central Minnesota, was three hundred dollars.

Then, from your own kitchen table, on a plain cream card with a real stamp — write him one page in your own hand.

Do not ask him for anything.

Say his mother's name once. Say Marian's name once. Say the November of 2005 once. Sign it *warmly.*

Ask, at the very end, whether he would drive up some Wednesday morning in October and talk to your incoming class for twenty minutes about the size of a small favor from the other side of a kitchen table.

Mail it Tuesday.

The auto-debit is going to run, the way it has run on the fifteenth of every month for one hundred and twenty-four months, on the fifteenth of the month after that, and the month after that, and the month after that.

Somebody's name is going to go on the plain white envelope in the top drawer of the sideboard.

Turn to him. ♡
